[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Rule the layer-zero grammar pair's held semantics during the migration

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-LAYER-ZERO-GRAMMAR-ADOPTION
TITLE: Rule the layer-zero grammar pair's held semantics during the migration
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A two-grammar sketch sits in packages/strictdoc-grammar/values/plan.nix and
values/spec.nix, wired to nothing: a `plan` vocabulary of PLAN and WORK, and a
`spec` vocabulary of REQUIREMENT, DECISION, MECHANISM, EVIDENCE and NARRATIVE.
The devenv block that would render them is present and commented out.

Written deliberately ahead of the system that would give them meaning, so the
node types could be argued about early. Each shape choice below therefore
carries a semantic commitment that was NOT ruled when it was written, and each
is held open here rather than settled in a comment nobody agreed to. Rule them
alongside the semantics migration, since that is the first point where any of
them can be decided against something real rather than against taste.

Held open, one line each:

- TIER duplicates what containment depth already implies. The redundancy is
  what makes "a tier must descend along Contained_By" decidable, but nothing
  says the redundancy is worth its cost.

- Whether an ungroomed item is a TIER value on the work node or an element type
  of its own. As drafted it is a tier, so the backlog is a query.

- Whether RATIONALE stays optional on DECISION. Drafted optional on the
  argument that a forced rationale restates the statement; the counter-argument
  is that an unexplained decision is the thing this node type exists to prevent.

- Which way Superseded_By points. Drafted forward, from the retired node to its
  replacement, so a reader arriving at a stale node is carried onward.

- Whether PLAN carries no containment edge, which is what makes the set of
  roots a query rather than a convention about where files sit.

- One state field per node type versus a tuple. Drafted as one, which keeps the
  first semantic layer a set of independent single-field ladders.

- Whether every type's state field should be called STATUS. Drafted that way,
  so one name carries four unrelated vocabularies; distinct names cost nothing
  now and are expensive later.

- Whether File relations stay absent. Drafted absent, because a path is an
  outside data source with a staleness story owed on day one.

- Whether this repository's own five node types migrate onto the pair, or the
  pair is abandoned in favour of the existing grammar.

One assumption is UNVERIFIED and load-bearing: the work node's Realizes role
points at a target in the other grammar, and nothing here has yet exercised a
relation whose two ends import different grammars. A two-file fixture settles
it, and it should be settled before anything is built on the split, because the
whole disposable-plan design rests on that edge.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The sketch was requested early on purpose: node types are cheap to argue about
in the abstract and expensive to change once a corpus exists. But writing the
grammar forces answers to questions the roadmap deliberately defers, and an
answer recorded only as a code comment is an answer nobody ruled on -- which is
the failure this graph exists to end.

Listing them as held rather than settled keeps the sketch useful without
letting it quietly become the design. It also gives the migration a checklist
instead of a rediscovery exercise.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Nothing imports either file, no check reads them, and the generation task will
not render them while the devenv block stays commented. Enabling them is a
decision, not a formality: neither has a corpus, and neither alias is
registered in the project configuration that resolves an imported grammar by
name.

The files carry plain-language descriptions of each node type's intended use,
and deliberately no design rationale -- that is what this node holds.
<<<
